    <desc>Enable, add, remove and reorder <app>Terminal</app> tabs.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Use tabs</title>

  <p>By default, <app>Terminal</app> lists tabs in the left sidebar, one
  title per row. Click a title to switch to that tab. Drag the divider to
  resize the sidebar. Its width is saved for new windows.</p>

  <p>Type a project name in <gui>Search projects</gui> at the top of the
  sidebar, or focus the field with
  <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>P</key></keyseq>.
  Use <key>Up</key> or <key>Down</key> to select a result and
  <key>Enter</key> to switch to an existing terminal. A collapsed result
  switches to its first terminal without expanding it. Press <key>Esc</key>
  to clear the search and return focus to the terminal. Searching never
  opens a new tab or changes which projects are collapsed.</p>

  <p>Use <gui>Add Working Directory…</gui> to choose a project directory,
  or click <gui>Add Current Directory</gui> next to it to save the active
  terminal's current local directory. Neither action opens a tab.
  Each project has two add buttons. <gui>New terminal in this project</gui>
  opens a single terminal. <gui>New split terminal tab in this project</gui>
  (the plus with a split icon) opens two independent terminals side by side,
  each taking half the available width. Both start in the project's
  directory and share one sidebar row.</p>

  <p>In a split tab, click either pane to direct typing and terminal
  shortcuts to it. Closing the tab closes both terminals. If one shell
  exits and its profile would normally close the terminal, that pane shows
  its exit status instead; the other terminal keeps running.</p>

  <p>Use <gui>Project options</gui> to change or remove a project directory,
  or to move a project up or down. Existing terminals stay open. The
  project order and collapsed state are saved between launches, but open
  terminals are not restored by the sidebar.</p>

  <p>Choose <gui>Open in Neovim</gui> from <gui>Project options</gui> to
  open the project directory in Neovim in a new tab. Neovim opens its
  built-in terminal in a bottom split, ready for input. The command
  requires <cmd>nvim</cmd> on the executable search path.</p>

  <p>Tabs are grouped under the most specific saved project that contains
  their starting directory. Changing directory inside a terminal does
  not move its tab. Click a project name to collapse or expand its tabs.
  The highlighted project contains the active terminal, even when collapsed.
  The number next to each project counts its open tabs.</p>

  <p>To cycle through tabs in the active tab's project, use
  <keyseq><key>Alt</key><key>Page Up</key></keyseq> or
  <keyseq><key>Alt</key><key>Page Down</key></keyseq>.
  These shortcuts can be changed in <gui>Preferences</gui>.</p>

  <section id="add-tab">
    <title>Open a new tab</title>

    <p>To open a new tab inside your current <app>Terminal</app> window:</p>
    <steps>
      <item>
        <p>Press
        <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>T</key></keyseq>.</p>
      </item>
    </steps>

  </section>

  <section id="rename-tab">
    <title>Rename a tab</title>

    <p>Click the <gui>Rename Tab</gui> button next to a tab in the sidebar,
    or choose <guiseq><gui style="menu">Terminal</gui>
    <gui style="menuitem">Rename Tab…</gui></guiseq> for the active tab.
    Enter a name and click <gui>Rename</gui>. Leave the field blank to
    restore the title supplied by the terminal.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="close-tab">
    <title>Remove a tab</title>

    <p>To close an existing tab inside your current <app>Terminal</app>
    window:</p>

    <steps>
      <item>
        <p>Select
        <guiseq><gui style="menu">File</gui><gui style="menuitem">Close
        Tab</gui></guiseq>.</p>
      </item>
    </steps>

    <p>You can also click the close button next to the terminal title in the
    sidebar.</p>

  </section>

  <section id="reorder-tab">
    <title>Reorder tabs</title>

    <p>To reorder the active tab, use <gui style="menu">Tabs</gui> →
    <gui style="menuitem">Move Tab Left</gui> or
    <gui style="menuitem">Move Tab Right</gui>. The sidebar keeps terminals
    grouped by project.</p>

  </section>

  <section id="move-tab-another-window">
    <title>Move a tab to another <app>Terminal</app> window</title>

    <p>If you want to move a tab from one window to another:</p>
    <steps>
      <item>
        <p>Click and hold the left mouse button on the tab.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>Drag the tab to the new window.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>Place it beside other tabs in the new window.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>Release the mouse button.</p>
      </item>
    </steps>

    <note style="tip">
      <p>You can move a tab from one window to another by dragging the tab to
      the <gui>Activities</gui> hot-corner of the <gui>GNOME Shell</gui>. This
      will reveal each of the open <app>Terminal</app> windows. You can release
      the tab that you are holding over the desired <app>Terminal</app>
      window.</p>
    </note>
  </section>

  <section id="move-tab-create-window">
    <title>Move a tab to create a new <app>Terminal</app> window</title>

    <p>To create a new window from an existing tab:</p>
    <steps>
      <item>
        <p>Click and hold the left mouse button on the tab.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>Drag the tab out of the current <app>Terminal</app> window.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>Release the mouse button.</p>
      </item>
    </steps>
  </section>

